.TH LX 1 "August 2026" "lx 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
lx \- power your Linux experience: system info, network, processes, packages, tuning, security, cleanup, services, backups, health, disk explorer, deep-dive, benchmarks, journal, cron, power
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lx
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] \fICOMMAND\fR [\fIARGS\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lx
is a modern, feature-rich CLI toolkit that bundles dozens of system
inspection, network, process, package, tweak, security, cleanup, service,
backup, health, disk-explorer, deep-dive, benchmark, journal, cron, and
power utilities behind a single colorful command.
.PP
Run
.B lx --help
for the command list and
.B lx COMMAND --help
for details on a specific command.
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Emit a machine-readable JSON envelope on stdout. The envelope has the stable
shape \fB{"tool", "version", "command", "timestamp", "data"}\fR; the payload
in \fBdata\fR is command-specific. Status messages are routed to stderr in
JSON mode so stdout stays parseable.
.TP
.B \-\-watch N
Repeat a read-only command every N seconds. Mutating commands reject
\fB--watch\fR with exit status 2 before anything runs. Combined with
\fB--json\fR, each tick is one newline-delimited JSON (NDJSON) document.
.TP
.B \-q, \-\-quiet
Suppress decorative output (headers, hints).
.TP
.B \-\-no-color
Disable ANSI colors. The \fBNO_COLOR\fR environment variable is honored too.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help and exit.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B info
Show system information: OS, kernel, CPU, memory, disk, GPU, network, uptime.
Flags: \fB--cpu\fR, \fB--mem\fR, \fB--disk\fR, \fB--gpu\fR, \fB--net\fR,
\fB--battery\fR, \fB--temps\fR, \fB--ip\fR, \fB--procs\fR, \fB--short\fR.
.TP
.B net
Network tools: \fBiface\fR (interfaces), \fBports\fR (listening sockets),
\fBip\fR (public IP), \fBspeed\fR (download speed test), \fBtrace\fR HOST
(traceroute), \fBping\fR HOST [-c N] (latency/jitter), \fBdns\fR HOST
(resolution + timing), \fBscan\fR (ARP/neighbour table), \fBall\fR.
.TP
.B proc
Process manager: \fBtop\fR [-n NUM] [-s cpu|mem|rss|time], \fBfind\fR NAME,
\fBtree\fR [NAME] [--depth N], \fBshow\fR PID
[\fB--no-threads\fR/\fB--no-env\fR/\fB--no-fd\fR/\fB--no-cwd\fR],
\fBkill\fR [-9] [-y] PATTERN, \fBme\fR. \fBkill\fR confirms before signaling.
.TP
.B pkg
Unified package manager wrapper for apt/dnf/pacman/zypper/apk/flatpak/snap:
\fBstatus\fR (per-backend counts), \fBlist\fR [-p GLOB], \fBinfo\fR P,
\fBsearch\fR QUERY, \fBupdate\fR, \fBupgrade\fR [--dry-run] [-y],
\fBinstall\fR NAME, \fBremove\fR NAME [-y], \fBpurge\fR, \fBorphans\fR,
\fBautoremove\fR [--dry-run] [-y]. \fBremove\fR and \fBautoremove\fR are
type-to-confirm (type \fBremove\fR). Backend auto-detected, override with
\fB-b/--backend\fR.
.TP
.B tweak
System tuning. \fBshow\fR (all block devices), \fBswappiness\fR N
[\fB--no-persist\fR], \fBmax-files\fR N, \fBgovernor\fR NAME, \fBbbr\fR,
\fBinteractive\fR, \fBnetwork\fR (curated network profile),
\fBswap create --size 2G\fR / \fBswap remove\fR (swapfile + fstab),
\fBtrim\fR [--dry-run] [-y] (fstrim -av), \fBrestore\fR [-y] (reset all
lx tuning; drop-in backed up; type \fBrestore\fR to confirm),
\fBset\fR KEY VALUE.
Most tweak subcommands require root.
.TP
.B sec
Security audit. \fBaudit\fR [--no-suid] [--format md|text], \fBssh\fR,
\fBports\fR, \fBsuid\fR, \fBusers\fR, \fBupdates\fR (security-flagged),
\fBworldwritable\fR, \fBsshkeys\fR, \fBhardssh\fR (writes hardening
drop-in).
.TP
.B clean
Free disk space. \fBcache\fR, \fBpip\fR, \fBdocker\fR, \fBsnap\fR, \fBtmp\fR
[\fB--days N\fR], \fBlogs\fR [--vacuum MB], \fBkernels\fR [--keep N],
\fBreport\fR (with recommendations). Destructive subcommands support
\fB--dry-run\fR and \fB-y\fR; root required for system-level ones.
\fBkernels\fR is type-to-confirm (type \fBpurge\fR).
.TP
.B service
systemd wrapper: \fBstatus\fR NAME, \fBlist\fR [--state S] [--type T],
\fBfailed\fR, \fBlog\fR NAME, \fBstart\fR/\fBstop\fR/\fBrestart\fR NAME,
\fBreload\fR NAME, \fBdaemon-reload\fR, \fBenable\fR/\fBdisable\fR NAME,
\fBmask\fR/\fBunmask\fR NAME [-y], \fBblame\fR [-n N], \fBboot\fR,
\fBtimers\fR. Group flag \fB--user\fR operates on user units (no root).
.TP
.B backup
Dotfile & config backup. \fBcreate\fR [-d DEST] [-t EXTRA]
[\fB--exclude\fR GLOB] [\fB--no-etc\fR], \fBlist\fR, \fBverify\fR ARCHIVE,
\fBprune\fR [\fB--keep N\fR] [--dry-run] [-y], \fBrestore\fR ARCHIVE
[\fB--dest\fR DIR] [--dry-run] [-y] (type \fBrestore\fR to confirm).
.TP
.B health
Compute an overall system health score (0-100) from weighted checks: CPU
load, memory, disk, failed units, connectivity, pending updates,
temperature, battery, swap, uptime, and zombies. Optional checks drop their
weight when unsupported. Filter with \fB--check\fR NAME[,NAME...]
(short names accepted).
.TP
.B doctor
Environment diagnostics: Python and dependency versions, root and sudo
access, systemd state, required and optional tools, PATH and permission
checks. Always exits 0.
.TP
.B fs
Disk explorer: \fBusage\fR [PATH] [-n N] [-d N] (largest directories),
\fBlarge\fR [PATH] [-n N] [\fB--min\fR MB] (largest files),
\fBinodes\fR [PATH] [-n N] [-d N] (inode pressure),
\fBmounts\fR (real filesystems with usage + inode stats).
Scans never follow symlinks and skip pseudo-filesystems.
.TP
.B sys
System deep-dive: \fBusb\fR, \fBpci\fR (sysfs fallbacks), \fBmodules\fR,
\fBenv\fR (secrets redacted), \fBtime\fR (NTP state + servers),
\fBboot\fR (boot history with durations), \fBall\fR.
.TP
.B bench
Pure-Python benchmarks, no root needed: \fBcpu\fR [-s SECS] [-t THREADS],
\fBmemory\fR [\fB--mb\fR N], \fBdisk\fR [\fB--mb\fR N] [\fB--path\fR DIR],
\fBall\fR. Each reports a 0-100 score against a reference desktop;
\fB--quick\fR shrinks workloads for fast smoke runs.
.TP
.B log
Journal explorer: \fBshow\fR [-n N] [\fB--since\fR S] [\fB--until\fR U]
[\fB-p\fR PRIORITY] [\fB-u\fR UNIT] [\fB--grep\fR RE], \fBerrors\fR
(error-priority entries, noisiest units first), \fBfollow\fR (live stream,
NDJSON in \fB--json\fR mode). Requires journalctl (systemd).
.TP
.B cron
Scheduling overview (read-only): \fBlist\fR [\fB--user\fR U] (your crontab,
/etc/crontab, /etc/cron.d; other users require root), \fBtimers\fR
(systemd timers).
.TP
.B power
Battery and power profiles: \fBbattery\fR (percent, charge rate in W, Wh
energy, design capacity, cycles, time left), \fBprofiles\fR (current +
available), \fBall\fR. All support \fB--watch\fR.
.TP
.B completion SHELL
Print a tab-completion script for \fBbash\fR, \fBzsh\fR, or \fBfish\fR.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Snapshot the system:
.B lx info
.TP
Machine-readable output piped into jq:
.B lx --json health | jq '.data.overall'
.TP
Live-refresh the health scoreboard every 2 seconds:
.B lx --watch 2 health
.TP
Show listening ports:
.B lx net ports -a
.TP
Top 20 processes by memory:
.B lx proc top -n 20 -s mem
.TP
Upgrade all packages (auto-detects apt on Debian/Ubuntu):
.B lx pkg upgrade -y
.TP
Apply desktop-friendly tuning (requires root):
.B sudo lx tweak interactive
.TP
Full security audit:
.B lx sec audit
.TP
Free up old kernels, keeping the newest 2 (requires root; type
\fBpurge\fR to confirm):
.B sudo lx clean kernels --keep 2
.TP
Back up dotfiles:
.B lx backup create
.TP
Check the environment:
.B lx doctor
.TP
See where disk space went:
.B lx fs usage
.TP
Errors from the last hour:
.B lx log errors --since "1h ago"
.TP
Quick benchmark:
.B lx bench all --quick
.TP
Cron jobs and systemd timers:
.B lx cron list
.TP
Battery and power profile:
.B lx power all
.TP
Install bash completion:
.B lx completion bash >> ~/.bashrc
.SH SAFETY
.B lx
follows a strict principle: inspection is free, mutation asks twice.
.PP
Read-only commands (info, net, proc, tweak show, pkg status, sec audit,
clean report, service status/list/failed/log/timers, backup list, health,
doctor, fs, sys, bench, log, cron, power) run as your normal user and
support \fB--watch\fR.
.PP
Mutating commands require root and confirm before acting. The most
destructive operations — \fBclean kernels\fR, \fBpkg remove\fR,
\fBpkg autoremove\fR, \fBtweak restore\fR, and \fBbackup restore\fR —
use "type-to-confirm": you must type the action word (\fBpurge\fR,
\fBremove\fR, or \fBrestore\fR) instead of answering y/N. Anything else,
including an empty line or end-of-input, aborts the operation (exit 130).
.PP
\fB--yes\fR/\fB-y\fR skips the prompt for scripting; in \fB--json\fR mode a
destructive run without \fB--yes\fR refuses with exit status 2 and a hint
on stderr, so scripts must opt in explicitly.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/sysctl.d/90-lx.conf
Persistent sysctl settings written by
.B lx tweak
commands (when \fB--persist\fR, the default).
.TP
.I /etc/security/limits.conf
Updated by
.B lx tweak max-files
.TP
.I /etc/ssh/sshd_config.d/99-lx-hardening.conf
Hardening drop-in written by
.B lx sec hardssh
.TP
.I ~/lx-backups/
Default destination for
.B lx backup create
.SH EXIT STATUS
Returns 0 on success, 1 on runtime errors, 2 on CLI usage errors, when
\fB--watch\fR is used on a mutating command, or when a destructive
\fB--json\fR run is refused for missing \fB--yes\fR, 130 on interrupt
(including a declined or wrong confirmation), and 124 if a subprocess
times out.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set (any value), disables ANSI colors (see https://no-color.org).
.TP
.B LX_WATCH_MAX_ITERS
Cap the number of \fB--watch\fR iterations (mainly useful in scripts and
tests).
.SH BUGS
Report issues via your distribution's bug tracker or the project's issue
tracker. Include the output of \fBlx --version\fR and the failing command.
